1
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:16,496
You know, I didn't set out
to be a parenting expert.

2
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:20,280
In fact, I'm not very interested
in parenting, per Se.

3
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:25,016
It's just that there's a certain style
of parenting these days

4
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:28,216
that is kind of messing up kids,

5
00:00:28,239 --> 00:00:33,296
impeding their chances
to develop into theirselves.

6
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:35,536
There's a certain style
of parenting these days

7
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:36,896
that's getting in the way.

8
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:38,536
I guess what I'm saying is,

9
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:40,616
we spend a lot of time
being very concerned

10
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:44,296
about parents who aren't involved enough
in the lives of their kids

11
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:46,256
and their education or their upbringing,

12
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:47,480
and rightly so.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,376
But at the other end of the spectrum,

14
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:53,096
there's a lot of harm
going on there as well,

15
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:56,056
where parents feel
a kid can't be successful

16
00:00:56,080 --> 00:01:00,136
unless the parent is protecting
and preventing at every turn

17
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:03,896
and hovering over every happening,
and micromanaging every moment,

18
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:09,120
and steering their kid towards
some small subset of colleges and careers.

19
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:13,376
When we raise kids this way,

20
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:15,096
and I'll say we,

21
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:17,896
because Lord knows,
in raising my two teenagers,

22
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:20,656
I've had these tendencies myself,

23
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:24,560
our kids end up leading
a kind of checklisted childhood.

24
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:27,896
And here's what the checklisted
childhood looks like.

25
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:30,816
We keep them safe and sound

26
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:32,520
and fed and watered,

27
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:36,216
and then we want to be sure
they go to the right schools,

28
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:38,816
that they're in the right classes
at the right schools,

29
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:42,460
and that they get the right grades
in the right classes in the right schools.

30
00:01:42,484 --> 00:01:44,216
But not just the grades, the scores,

31
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:47,456
and not just the grades and scores,
but the accolades and the awards

32
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:49,671
and the sports,
the activities, the leadership.

33
00:01:49,695 --> 00:01:51,616
We tell our kids, don't just join a club,

34
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:53,896
start a club, because colleges
want to see that.

35
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:55,856
And check the box for community service.

36
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:58,136
I mean, show the colleges
you care about others.

37
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:00,256
(Laughter)

38
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:05,216
And all of this is done to some
hoped-for degree of perfection.

39
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:07,856
We expect our kids
to perform at a level of perfection

40
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:11,216
we were never asked
to perform at ourselves,

41
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:13,416
and so because so much is required,

42
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:14,656
we think,

43
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:17,896
well then, of course we parents
have to argue with every teacher

44
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:20,496
and principal and coach and referee

45
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:23,416
and act like our kid's concierge

46
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:24,680
and personal handler

47
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:27,696
and secretary.

48
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:29,896
And then with our kids, our precious kids,

49
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:32,056
we spend so much time nudging,

50
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:36,496
cajoling, hinting, helping, haggling,
nagging as the case may be,

51
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:39,376
to be sure they're not screwing up,

52
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:41,896
not closing doors,

53
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:44,416
not ruining their future,

54
00:02:44,440 --> 00:02:46,696
some hoped-for admission

55
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:49,336
to a tiny handful of colleges

56
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:52,440
that deny almost every applicant.

57
00:02:54,680 --> 00:03:00,096
And here's what it feels like
to be a kid in this checklisted childhood.

58
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:02,616
First of all, there's
no time for free play.

59
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:04,296
There's no room in the afternoons,

60
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:07,336
because everything
has to be enriching, we think.

61
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:10,736
It's as if every piece of homework,
every quiz, every activity

62
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:15,296
is a make-or-break moment
for this future we have in mind for them,

63
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:18,256
and we absolve them
of helping out around the house,

64
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:21,656
and we even absolve them
of getting enough sleep

65
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:26,576
as long as they're checking off
the items on their checklist.

66
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:30,416
And in the checklisted childhood,
we say we just want them to be happy,

67
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:32,496
but when they come home from school,

68
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:35,656
what we ask about all too often first

69
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:38,736
is their homework and their grades.

70
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:40,776
And they see in our faces

71
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:43,336
that our approval, that our love,

72
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:44,976
that their very worth,

73
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:46,856
comes from A's.

74
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:49,456
And then we walk alongside them

75
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:54,176
and offer clucking praise like a trainer
at the Westminster Dog Show --

76
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:55,416
(Laughter)

77
00:03:55,440 --> 00:04:00,736
coaxing them to just jump a little higher
and soar a little farther,

78
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:03,816
day after day after day.

79
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:05,576
And when they get to high school,

80
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:08,496
they don't say, "Well, what might I
be interested in studying

81
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:09,776
or doing as an activity?"

82
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:11,496
They go to counselors and they say,

83
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:15,096
"What do I need to do
to get into the right college?"

84
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:18,056
And then, when the grades
start to roll in in high school,

85
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:19,656
and they're getting some B's,

86
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:22,056
or God forbid some C's,

87
00:04:22,080 --> 00:04:24,016
they frantically text their friends

88
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:29,160
and say, "Has anyone ever gotten
into the right college with these grades?"

89
00:04:29,959 --> 00:04:31,495
And our kids,

90
00:04:31,519 --> 00:04:35,136
regardless of where they end up
at the end of high school,

91
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:36,400
they're breathless.

92
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:38,616
They're brittle.

93
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:40,456
They're a little burned out.

94
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:42,376
They're a little old before their time,

95
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:46,416
wishing the grown-ups in their lives
had said, "What you've done is enough,

96
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:49,416
this effort you've put forth
in childhood is enough."

97
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:53,696
And they're withering now
under high rates of anxiety and depression

98
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:55,936
and some of them are wondering,

99
00:04:55,960 --> 00:05:00,320
will this life ever turn out
to have been worth it?

100
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:03,536
Well, we parents,

101
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:06,936
we parents are pretty sure
it's all worth it.

102
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:08,176
We seem to behave --

103
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:10,936
it's like we literally think
they will have no future

104
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:15,656
if they don't get into one of these
tiny set of colleges or careers

105
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:17,000
we have in mind for them.

106
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:21,016
Or maybe, maybe, we're just afraid

107
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:23,376
they won't have a future we can brag about

108
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:27,200
to our friends and with stickers
on the backs of our cars.

109
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:30,816
Yeah.

110
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:33,160
(Applause)

111
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:39,456
But if you look at what we've done,

112
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:43,016
if you have the courage
to really look at it,

113
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:46,016
you'll see that not only do our kids
think their worth comes

114
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:47,736
from grades and scores,

115
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:51,656
but that when we live right up inside
their precious developing minds

116
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:56,256
all the time, like our very own version
of the movie "Being John Malkovich,"

117
00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:58,216
we send our children the message:

118
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:03,456
"Hey kid, I don't think you can actually
achieve any of this without me."

119
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:06,136
And so with our overhelp,

120
00:06:06,160 --> 00:06:08,696
our overprotection
and overdirection and hand-holding,

121
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:12,736
we deprive our kids
of the chance to build self-efficacy,

122
00:06:12,760 --> 00:06:16,456
which is a really fundamental tenet
of the human psyche,

123
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:19,096
far more important
than that self-esteem they get

124
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:20,520
every time we applaud.

125
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:27,296
Self-efficacy is built when one sees
that one's own actions lead to outcomes,

126
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:28,536
not --

127
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:29,776
There you go.

128
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:32,640
(Applause)

129
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:37,376
Not one's parents'
actions on one's behalf,

130
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:40,176
but when one's own actions
lead to outcomes.

131
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:41,696
So simply put,

132
00:06:41,720 --> 00:06:47,056
if our children are to develop
self-efficacy, and they must,

133
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:51,936
then they have to do a whole lot more
of the thinking, planning, deciding,

134
00:06:51,960 --> 00:06:55,536
doing, hoping, coping, trial and error,

135
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:58,696
dreaming and experiencing of life

136
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:00,360
for themselves.

137
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:03,776
Now, am I saying

138
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:06,136
every kid is hard-working and motivated

139
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:09,336
and doesn't need a parent's involvement
or interest in their lives,

140
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:11,240
and we should just back off and let go?

141
00:07:12,040 --> 00:07:13,256
Hell no.

142
00:07:13,280 --> 00:07:14,496
(Laughter)

143
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:16,216
That is not what I'm saying.

144
00:07:16,240 --> 00:07:19,856
What I'm saying is, when we treat
grades and scores and accolades and awards

145
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:21,976
as the purpose of childhood,

146
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,776
all in furtherance of some hoped-for
admission to a tiny number of colleges

147
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:28,776
or entrance to a small number of careers,

148
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:33,176
that that's too narrow a definition
of success for our kids.

149
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:36,736
And even though we might help them
achieve some short-term wins

150
00:07:36,760 --> 00:07:37,976
by overhelping --

151
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,560
like they get a better grade
if we help them do their homework,

152
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:46,200
they might end up with a longer
childhood résumé when we help --

153
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:50,496
what I'm saying is that all of this
comes at a long-term cost

154
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:52,576
to their sense of self.

155
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:55,016
What I'm saying is,
we should be less concerned

156
00:07:55,040 --> 00:07:56,856
with the specific set of colleges

157
00:07:56,880 --> 00:08:00,096
they might be able
to apply to or might get into

158
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:05,016
and far more concerned that they have
the habits, the mindset, the skill set,

159
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:09,976
the wellness, to be successful
wherever they go.

160
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:11,216
What I'm saying is,

161
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:15,816
our kids need us to be a little
less obsessed with grades and scores

162
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:18,136
and a whole lot more interested

163
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:23,256
in childhood providing
a foundation for their success

164
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:25,960
built on things like love

165
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:28,456
and chores.

166
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:30,656
(Laughter)

167
00:08:30,680 --> 00:08:32,600
(Applause)

168
00:08:35,240 --> 00:08:38,480
Did I just say chores?
Did I just say chores? I really did.

169
00:08:39,920 --> 00:08:42,080
But really, here's why.

170
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:46,936
The longest longitudinal study
of humans ever conducted

171
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:49,135
is called the Harvard Grant Study.

172
00:08:49,159 --> 00:08:51,496
It found that professional
success in life,

173
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:54,096
which is what we want for our kids,

174
00:08:54,120 --> 00:08:57,896
that professional success in life
comes from having done chores as a kid,

175
00:08:57,920 --> 00:08:59,936
and the earlier you started, the better,

176
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:02,336
that a roll-up-your-sleeves-
and-pitch-in mindset,

177
00:09:02,360 --> 00:09:04,736
a mindset that says,
there's some unpleasant work,

178
00:09:04,760 --> 00:09:06,976
someone's got to do it,
it might as well be me,

179
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:08,216
a mindset that says,

180
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:11,336
I will contribute my effort
to the betterment of the whole,

181
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:14,416
that that's what gets you ahead
in the workplace.

182
00:09:14,440 --> 00:09:17,336
Now, we all know this. You know this.

183
00:09:17,360 --> 00:09:20,176
(Applause)

184
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:23,776
We all know this, and yet,
in the checklisted childhood,

185
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:27,576
we absolve our kids of doing
the work of chores around the house,

186
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:30,096
and then they end up
as young adults in the workplace

187
00:09:30,120 --> 00:09:32,336
still waiting for a checklist,

188
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:33,816
but it doesn't exist,

189
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:37,136
and more importantly,
lacking the impulse, the instinct

190
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:39,376
to roll up their sleeves and pitch in

191
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:42,976
and look around and wonder,
how can I be useful to my colleagues?

192
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,320
How can I anticipate a few steps ahead
to what my boss might need?

193
00:09:48,280 --> 00:09:51,880
A second very important finding
from the Harvard Grant Study

194
00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:54,680
said that happiness in life

195
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:57,576
comes from love,

196
00:09:57,600 --> 00:09:59,456
not love of work,

197
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:01,680
love of humans:

198
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:05,840
our spouse, our partner,
our friends, our family.

199
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:10,296
So childhood needs to teach
our kids how to love,

200
00:10:10,320 --> 00:10:13,296
and they can't love others
if they don't first love themselves,

201
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:17,296
and they won't love themselves
if we can't offer them unconditional love.

202
00:10:17,320 --> 00:10:19,280
(Applause)

203
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:22,800
Right.

204
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:26,016
And so,

205
00:10:26,040 --> 00:10:28,296
instead of being obsessed
with grades and scores

206
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:31,216
when our precious offspring
come home from school,

207
00:10:31,240 --> 00:10:32,736
or we come home from work,

208
00:10:32,760 --> 00:10:35,536
we need to close our technology,
put away our phones,

209
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:36,936
and look them in the eye

210
00:10:36,960 --> 00:10:40,136
and let them see
the joy that fills our faces

211
00:10:40,160 --> 00:10:43,296
when we see our child
for the first time in a few hours.

212
00:10:43,320 --> 00:10:45,256
And then we have to say,

213
00:10:45,280 --> 00:10:46,480
"How was your day?

214
00:10:47,840 --> 00:10:50,656
What did you like about today?"

215
00:10:50,680 --> 00:10:54,416
And when your teenage daughter
says, "Lunch," like mine did,

216
00:10:54,440 --> 00:10:56,496
and I want to hear about the math test,

217
00:10:56,520 --> 00:10:57,936
not lunch,

218
00:10:57,960 --> 00:11:00,976
you have to still
take an interest in lunch.

219
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,176
You gotta say, "What was great
about lunch today?"

220
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:07,576
They need to know
they matter to us as humans,

221
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:10,200
not because of their GPA.

222
00:11:11,640 --> 00:11:13,856
All right, so you're thinking,
chores and love,

223
00:11:13,880 --> 00:11:16,376
that sounds all well and good,
but give me a break.

224
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:19,376
The colleges want to see
top scores and grades

225
00:11:19,400 --> 00:11:23,320
and accolades and awards,
and I'm going to tell you, sort of.

226
00:11:25,080 --> 00:11:30,976
The very biggest brand-name schools
are asking that of our young adults,

227
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:32,360
but here's the good news.

228
00:11:33,160 --> 00:11:38,256
Contrary to what the college
rankings racket would have us believe --

229
00:11:38,280 --> 00:11:40,640
(Applause)

230
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:46,936
you don't have to go to one
of the biggest brand name schools

231
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:48,696
to be happy and successful in life.

232
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:51,576
Happy and successful people
went to state school,

233
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:53,856
went to a small college
no one has heard of,

234
00:11:53,880 --> 00:11:55,456
went to community college,

235
00:11:55,480 --> 00:11:58,216
went to a college over here
and flunked out.

236
00:11:58,240 --> 00:12:01,040
(Applause)

237
00:12:05,240 --> 00:12:08,216
The evidence is in this room,
is in our communities,

238
00:12:08,240 --> 00:12:09,936
that this is the truth.

239
00:12:09,960 --> 00:12:11,656
And if we could widen our blinders

240
00:12:11,680 --> 00:12:13,936
and be willing to look
at a few more colleges,

241
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:17,496
maybe remove our own egos
from the equation,

242
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:21,176
we could accept and embrace
this truth and then realize,

243
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:23,176
it is hardly the end of the world

244
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:26,720
if our kids don't go to one
of those big brand-name schools.

245
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:29,216
And more importantly,

246
00:12:29,240 --> 00:12:34,336
if their childhood has not been lived
according to a tyrannical checklist

247
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:36,496
then when they get to college,

248
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:38,456
whichever one it is,

249
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:41,576
well, they'll have gone there
on their own volition,

250
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:44,056
fueled by their own desire,

251
00:12:44,080 --> 00:12:47,280
capable and ready to thrive there.

252
00:12:49,360 --> 00:12:51,160
I have to admit something to you.

253
00:12:51,760 --> 00:12:54,856
I've got two kids I mentioned,
Sawyer and Avery.

254
00:12:54,880 --> 00:12:56,576
They're teenagers.

255
00:12:56,600 --> 00:12:58,456
And once upon a time,

256
00:12:58,480 --> 00:13:01,096
I think I was treating my Sawyer and Avery

257
00:13:01,120 --> 00:13:02,896
like little bonsai trees --

258
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:05,256
(Laughter)

259
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:08,216
that I was going
to carefully clip and prune

260
00:13:08,240 --> 00:13:12,136
and shape into some perfect
form of a human

261
00:13:12,160 --> 00:13:15,656
that might just be perfect enough
to warrant them admission

262
00:13:15,680 --> 00:13:18,120
to one of the most
highly selective colleges.

263
00:13:19,040 --> 00:13:22,920
But I've come to realize, after working
with thousands of other people's kids --

264
00:13:23,640 --> 00:13:25,360
(Laughter)

265
00:13:26,480 --> 00:13:28,800
and raising two kids of my own,

266
00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:32,400
my kids aren't bonsai trees.

267
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:35,520
They're wildflowers

268
00:13:36,400 --> 00:13:39,216
of an unknown genus and species --

269
00:13:39,240 --> 00:13:41,136
(Laughter)

270
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:44,576
and it's my job to provide
a nourishing environment,

271
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:46,976
to strengthen them through chores

272
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:51,296
and to love them so they can
love others and receive love

273
00:13:51,320 --> 00:13:54,416
and the college, the major, the career,

274
00:13:54,440 --> 00:13:56,136
that's up to them.

275
00:13:56,160 --> 00:14:01,816
My job is not to make them become
what I would have them become,

276
00:14:01,840 --> 00:14:06,640
but to support them
in becoming their glorious selves.

277
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:08,616
Thank you.

278
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:15,070
(Applause)
